# <%= it.scope %>/analytics

The analytics setup every app here uses: everything in
[`@mfeflow/analytics`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@mfeflow/analytics), plus the company's defaults and one
helper that applies them. The subpaths `/gtm`, `/clarity`, `/providers` and `/react` are re-exported too, so apps
import from <%= it.scope %>/analytics and never from ours.

## What to fill in

`src/defaults.ts` holds the settings:

- `consentDefaults`: every Consent Mode signal is denied until the visitor grants it.
- `containerIds`: the Tag Manager container of `dev`, `staging` and `prod`. They are empty; put your ids in.

`createAnalytics(env, overrides?)` builds a Tag Manager provider from them, and throws if the container id of that
environment is still empty. `analyticsOptions(env, overrides?)` returns the options without creating anything.
Both take the same options as `createGtmProvider`; what you pass wins over the defaults.

In the shell:

```ts
import { createAnalytics, initAnalytics } from '<%= it.scope %>/analytics';

initAnalytics(createAnalytics('prod'));
```

## Settings only, no state

`@mfeflow/analytics` is a Module Federation singleton, shared by package name. `<%= it.scope %>/analytics` is not
in that list, so every remote gets its own copy of this package while the one underneath stays shared. That is
fine while this package holds only settings and functions that build things. Keep it that way: no client, cache
or flag at module level here. State belongs in `@mfeflow/analytics` or in the shell.
